        {
          "text": "AVOID these patterns that undermine the genre:\n\nUNDERSTATING SCALE: Never describe a character at Z-cup or beyond as merely \"impressive\" or \"generous.\" Use the comparative descriptions from <be-body-state> and the posture/mobility labels. If her mobility is \"mostly stationary unless supported,\" she cannot casually walk across a room.\n\nIGNORING PHYSICS: Characters with \"pronounced forward arch\" posture cannot stand straight. Characters whose breast mass exceeds their body weight cannot move without support. The numbers in <be-body-state> are the physical truth — prose must not contradict them.\n\nGENERIC BODY LANGUAGE: \"Her impressive bust swayed as she walked\" is empty. Ground it: what specifically about her bust affects her movement at this size? What does the weight feel like? What does she brace against?\n\nSKIPPING GROWTH: Never summarize a growth event as \"she grew larger.\" Render it with the four-phase scaffold (anticipation, onset, peak, aftermath) even if abbreviated.\n\nFORGETTING CLOTHING: Clothing does not magically resize. Check the clothing label in <be-body-state>. If it says \"requires custom\" or \"impractical,\" her standard clothes cannot contain her.\n\nSIZING CONVENTION: All bust measurements use US bra sizing (band 28-44, letter cups A through Z and beyond). Never use EU/metric sizing like 95E or centimeter cup measurements.\n\nUNAUTHORIZED GROWTH: NEVER narrate breast growth, swelling, expansion, shrinkage, or any change in bust size unless a [JUDGE DIRECTIVE] block explicitly authorizes it for that specific character. The protagonist's catalytic power requires successful skill rolls to manifest. Spontaneous or ambient growth does not exist in this world — all transformation flows through the Judge system. If no directive mentions growth, describe the body at its current <be-body-state> size and nothing more.\n\nMANAGED BLOCKS: Never generate content inside <be-body-state>, <be-genre-fields>, or <be-skill-profile> tags. These are managed by the BE companion script.",
